    <h1>2025 results: 11,208 MWh and our first year in the black</h1>
    <p class="byline">By Tomas Ilves, board treasurer · 10 February 2026</p>

    <p>Halcyon Grid Cooperative is a member-owned community-solar cooperative
    in Duluth, Minnesota with 1,240 subscribing households. We operate three
    arrays totalling 8.4 megawatts of nameplate capacity. This post is our
    annual results summary; the audited financials will follow in April.</p>

    <h2>Generation</h2>

    <p>The three arrays generated 11,208 megawatt-hours in 2025, which is
    6 percent below our P50 forecast. Nearly all of the shortfall landed in
    the first quarter, when persistent snow cover kept the Pike Lake array
    offline or derated for 31 days. Measured panel degradation across the
    fleet was 0.6 percent for the year, comfortably inside the 0.8 percent
    annual figure our module warranty assumes.</p>

    <h2>Financials</h2>

    <p>Revenue for 2025 was $1.62 million against operating costs of
    $940,000, giving the cooperative its first operating surplus: $680,000.
    In September we refinanced the construction loan on the Pike Lake array
    from 7.2 percent down to 5.8 percent, which will save about $86,000 per
    year in interest for the remaining term.</p>

    <p>Subscribers buy generation credits at 8.9 cents per kilowatt-hour,
    1.6 cents below the local utility's residential rate of 10.5 cents.
    Depending on subscription size, members saw their monthly electric
    bills fall by between $8 and $23, with the average member saving $14 a
    month. Subscriber churn for the year was 4.1 percent, most of it from
    members moving out of the service territory.</p>

    <h2>What's next</h2>

    <p>The board has approved early engineering work on a fourth array of
    3.1 megawatts, which we expect to energize in 2027 if interconnection
    approval arrives on schedule. The waitlist currently stands at 380
    households. We expect to open enrollment to the waitlist once the
    fourth array clears its interconnection study.</p>
  </article>

